course: world
lesson: 1
title: Day and night
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Earth is a huge ball that spins around an imaginary line through its middle, called its axis.
explain: The Sun shines on only one half of the ball at a time: that half has day, and the other half, turned away from the Sun, has night.
explain: As the Earth spins, every place is carried from the dark half into the light half and back again, once in 24 hours.
explain: The Sun seems to rise in the east and set in the west because the Earth turns from west to east, the way trees seem to run backwards when you look out of a moving train.
explain: Try it with a torch and a ball in a dark room: spin the ball slowly and watch a sticker on it pass from light into shadow.
fact: The Earth spins around its axis once in about 24 hours.
fact: At any moment one half of the Earth is lit by the Sun and has day.
fact: The half of the Earth turned away from the Sun has night.
fact: The Sun does not go around the Earth to make day and night.
fact: The Earth turns from west to east.
fact: Because of the turning, the Sun seems to rise in the east and set in the west.
fact: When it is midday in one country, it is midnight on the opposite side of the Earth.
fact: The Moon and the stars also seem to cross the sky because the Earth turns.
fact: The stars are in the sky during the day too, but the bright sunlight hides them.
fact: The line between the lit half and the dark half moves around the Earth as it spins, bringing sunrise and sunset.
exercise: Why do we have day and night? | Because the Earth turns around its axis | Because the Sun goes around the Earth every day | Because the Moon covers the Sun at night | Because the Sun switches off at night
exercise: How long does the Earth take to spin around once? | About 24 hours | About twelve hours | About one whole week | About 365 days, a year
exercise: What is the axis of the Earth? | An imaginary line through its middle that it spins around | A real metal rod going through the Earth from pole to pole | The path the Earth follows around the Sun | The line on maps that splits the Earth into two halves
exercise: In which direction does the Sun seem to rise? | In the east | In the far west | In the north | In the south
exercise: In which direction does the Earth turn? | From west to east | From east to west | From north to south | From south to north
exercise: Why does the Sun seem to move across the sky? | Because the Earth is turning under it | Because the Sun goes around the Earth | Because the wind pushes the Sun along | Because the Sun follows the Moon
exercise: How much of the Earth has day at any one moment? | About one half | All of it | About one quarter | Only the part near the equator
exercise: Why is it night in your town? | Your side of the Earth is turned away from the Sun | The Sun has gone out for a few hours | The Moon is standing between you and the Sun | Clouds have covered the Sun everywhere
exercise: Where are the stars during the day? | Still in the sky, hidden by the bright sunlight | Below the ground until the evening | Gone out until the night comes | Behind the Sun all the time, every day of the year
exercise: When it is midday in one country, what time is it on the opposite side of the Earth? | About midnight | Also midday | Early morning | Late afternoon
exercise: What does the Sun do while the Earth spins? | It keeps shining while the Earth turns in its light | It goes around the Earth once a day | It goes out every evening | It moves from the west to the east of the sky every day
exercise: Why does the Moon also seem to cross the night sky? | Because the Earth is turning | Because the Moon is pulled by the clouds | Because the Moon follows the Sun around the Earth each day | Because the Moon is blown by the wind
exercise: In a model with a torch and a ball, what does the torch stand for? | The Sun | The Moon | The Earth | A star at night
exercise: What brings a place from night into morning? | The Earth turning it into the sunlight | The Sun coming back from under the Earth | The Moon moving away from the Sun | The clouds opening up in the east
test: If the Earth stopped spinning, what would happen to day and night? | One side would have a very long day and the other a very long night | Nothing would change at all | It would be night everywhere at the same time | The days would become much shorter
test: Anna in a dark room shines a torch at a slowly spinning ball with a sticker on it. What happens to the sticker? | It passes into the light and then into the shadow again and again | It stays in the light all the time | It stays in the shadow all the time | It lights up only when the ball stops
test: Which is true? | The Sun seems to move because the Earth turns | The Sun really moves across the sky every day | The Sun goes behind the Earth to sleep | The Sun moves faster in the evening
test: Sam sees the Sun setting. Which way is he looking? | West | East | North | Straight up
test: A friend in a far country is having breakfast while you go to bed. Why? | Her side of the Earth is turning into sunlight while yours turns away | Her country is closer to the Sun than yours | The Sun visits her country after yours | Her country has a different Sun
test: How many times does a place on the Earth pass from day into night in one week? | Seven times | Once | Fourteen times | Twice
test: Why can you not see stars at noon? | Sunlight lights up the sky and hides them | The stars move to the other side of the Earth | The stars switch off during the day | The Moon hides the stars in the daytime
test: Which picture of day and night is right? | A spinning ball lit from one side by a lamp | A still ball with a lamp going around it daily | A ball that is lit from all sides at once | A ball whose lamp is turned on and off
test: If you stood on the line between the lit and dark halves of the Earth, what would you see? | The Sun low at the edge of the sky, rising or setting | The Sun straight above your head | The Moon covering the Sun | No light at all
test: why's the sky go dark at night anyway | Our side of the Earth has turned away from the Sun | The Sun goes behind the mountains to rest for the night | The Moon blocks the Sun every evening | The Sun runs out of light at the end of the day
test: if its lunch time here wots it like on the other side of the world | It's about the middle of the night there | It's lunch time there too | It's always morning there | It's the day before there, at lunch time
test: Leo says the Sun goes round the Earth every day and that's why it gets dark. true? | No, the Earth spins and turns us away from the Sun | Yes, the Sun circles the Earth once every day | Yes, but only in winter does it go round | No, the Moon goes round and covers the Sun
test: my sister reckons the stars only come out at night cos they switch on. right? | No, they're there all day but sunlight hides them | Yes, stars light up only when it gets dark outside | Yes, stars turn on when the Sun turns off | No, the stars hide under the Earth by day
exam: Identify the principal cause of the alternation of day and night upon the Earth. | The rotation of the Earth upon its own axis | The daily revolution of the Sun about the Earth | The interposition of the Moon between Earth and Sun | The periodic extinguishing of the Sun's illumination
exam: It is given that the Earth completes one rotation upon its axis. State the approximate duration of the said rotation. | Twenty-four hours | Twelve hours | Seven days | Three hundred and sixty-five days
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the apparent diurnal motion of the Sun? | It appears to rise in the east and set in the west owing to the Earth's rotation | It actually travels from east to west about a stationary Earth | It appears to rise in the west and set in the east | It moves eastward across the heavens each morning
exam: Whereas a given locality experiences midday, ascertain the approximate hour at the diametrically opposite point of the globe. | Approximately midnight | Likewise midday, at the same hour | Early morning, about dawn | Late afternoon, before dusk
